  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday and Friday: A cold front will continue to sag southward\nslowly today, bringing breezy west winds around 10-20 mph with\ngusts around 25 mph. There is a chance for blowing dust across\nthe Columbia Basin but with the wind forecast trending down, not\nexpecting widespread impacts. Little precipitation will come\nfrom this front besides some sprinkles and light showers late\nthis evening in southeast Washington and the central and\nsouthern Idaho Panhandle. There is a small chance (5%) of\nisolated lightning with these showers with up to 200 J/kg CAPE\nfrom the most unstable parcel originating near the 700 mb warm\nnose on forecast soundings. Temperatures Friday behind the cold\nfront will return to below average in the 60s to mid 70s. Light\nupslope fall into the northern ID Panhandle will bring a chance\n(15-30%) for surface showers and isolated thunderstorms Friday\nafternoon. Forecast instability looks very weak again tomorrow \naround 100-200 J/kg so not expecting widespread lightning.\n\nSaturday through Thursday: Larger changes arrive Saturday\nafternoon as a deep trough strengthens off the coast. Much of\nthe day will be fairly nice with highs in the 60s and 70s with\nbreezy northeast winds in the Columbia Basin. Ensembles suggest\nPWATs will increase from near normal in the morning to around\n120-180 percent of normal by Saturday evening. Precipitation\nchances will increase from south to north as the atmosphere\nsaturates in the evening. There are still substantial\ndifferences in the QPF amounts amongst the ensembles but the\noverall trend is a higher chance of 0.5 inches or more of rain\nthrough Monday morning, especially outside of southeast\nWashington and the southern ID Panhandle. For example, yesterday\nmorning\u2019s NBM run only had a 34 percent chance of 0.5+ inches\nfor Spokane while this morning\u2019s run increased to 62 percent.\nOverall, these chances for central Washington are around 30-45%,\n50-70% for eastern Washington (greatest chances near the Idaho\nborder), and 60-75% for the Idaho Panhandle. There is a small\nchance of thunderstorms Sunday afternoon near the\nIdaho/Washington border and Idaho Sunday as the cold pool aloft\npasses through the region. Forecast instability looks very weak\nwith fairly stable mid levels, but surface heating may be\nsufficient to get a couple strikes. Temperatures Sunday will be\nwell below normal in the 50s and 60s. Monday onward will bring a\nwarming and drying trend as the low departs. There are some\ndisagreements amongst the ensembles of additional troughing into\nTuesday which would bring additional showery conditions. As\nridging returns mid next week, temperatures will return into the\n70s and 80s. /Butler",
